we all know that apple is the world's  largest company the world's most  profitable company and arguably the  world's most revolutionary company but  did you know that apple is also one of  the most indebted companies  as of june 2022 apple had 10.9 billion  dollars in outstanding commercial paper  14 billion worth of short-term debt and  48.8 billion dollars in other current  liabilities they also had a total of  94.7 billion in long-term debt and 53.6  billion dollars in other non-current  liabilities all of this adds up to a  total of 222 billion dollars worth of  debt given apple's insane profitability  and their massive cash reserves you  would think that apple would pay off the  dead asap but they've actually been  doing the exact opposite over the past  several years apple has been  consistently taking on new debt if you  take a look at their total liabilities  over time you'll see that they've 10x  their debt over the past decade and just  a few weeks ago they raised another 5.5  billion dollars at the same time apple  has also been vigorously reducing their  cash reserves since the beginning of  2020 apple has reduced their cash  reserve from 107 billion dollars to just  under 50 billion dollars so while apple  is indeed more profitable than ever  before they're also more leveraged than  ever before with a debt equity ratio of  over two so why in the world does apple  have so much debt  starting off with the reasoning that  applies to not just apple but all of us  we have the economic reasoning by this  point i'm sure all of you guys are well  aware of how high inflation is in july  we saw the inflation reading peak above  nine percent and in the most recent  inflation reading we saw inflation come  in at eight point six percent people are  hopeful that this decline means that the  peak for inflation is already in it but  even if that's true we're likely going  to see elevated inflation readings for  at least another year so it's smart to  leverage strategies that benefit from  high inflation and one of those  strategies is taking on more debt now at  first this may seem a bit  counterintuitive when inflation is high  the cost of production skyrockets which  makes it difficult for companies to  maintain their margins so  psychologically the last thing you'd  want to do is add on more debt and take  on yet another regular payment but in  the long term this is actually the best  time to be taking out debt you see if  you lock in a loan today your payments  themselves will be the same for the  entire duration of the loan however this  cannot be said about the value of your  payments with high inflation the real  value of each payment will be  consistently declining paying a thousand  dollar mortgage in 1990 is not the same  as paying a thousand dollar mortgage in  2022. the 2022 mortgage is far cheaper  despite being the same dollar value  companies are able to take this idea to  even the next level because their debt  generally works differently from the  debt that we're used to for example with  a mortgage you'll be paying principal  and interest every single month interest  does make up a larger portion at the  beginning and a smaller portion near the  end but regardless you're paying  interest and principal with every  payment with corporate debt however  companies generally only make interest  payments for the duration of the debt  and it ends not till the end of the term  that companies pay back the principal  now this might not sound like a big deal  but it is and here's why  let's say apple took out one dollar  worth of debt in 1970 at a three percent  interest rate for 30 years  interest rates were obviously not that  low in the 1970s but that was the last  time that we saw inflation at these  levels so that's why we're using this  time period  anyway this would mean that apple has to  pay 3 cents per year in interest which  works out to a total of 90 cents over 30  years this is quite a substantial amount  given that the interest itself is nearly  equivalent to the initial loan value of  one dollar but despite this apple would  actually come out ahead by quite a  margin you see apple didn't have to pay  back the initial one dollar till 2000  and by that point one two thousand  dollar was only worth 23 1970 cents so  essentially apple borrowed a dollar and  only paid back 23 cents you would of  course also have to factor in the cost  of the interest but the point is that  apple comes out on top by a large amount  considering this you might be asking why  in the world would anybody loan apple  any money when they know that apple is  going to come out ahead well the answer  is that the lender's alternatives are  even worse usually the people that are  lending these companies money are banks  pension funds and conservative  individuals who are retiring soon for  these people stability and liquidity are  extremely important so they can't just  go out and invest in stocks  so really they only have two options  which are to either earn three percent  from apple microsoft and other large  corporations or earn half a percent from  money market funds and cds or maybe two  percent from treasuries and from this  perspective taking the three percent  deal is a no-brainer so really it's a  win-win scenario apple gets cheap money  and while lenders get the best returns  they can without going to equities  [Music]  as you can see borrowing money at low  interest rates during high inflation  itself is rather profitable but apple  isn't going around and borrowing money  just so they can turn interest rates  honestly that's probably not even worth  the hassle for them the main reason  they're borrowing so much money is to  actually deploy it in areas that produce  greater returns for apple specifically  this seems to be in two areas starting  with stock buybacks if you're not  familiar with stock buybacks this is  essentially when a company goes onto the  stock market and buys their own shares  this not only increases demand for apple  stock but it also reduces the supply of  apple stock you see it when a company  buys back their own shares these shares  become what is known as treasury stock  at this point companies do have the  right to sell these shares back on the  market if they wanted to  but more times than not companies decide  to retire these shares which basically  means to remove them from the supply  permanently over the past several years  apple has been buying back record  amounts of shares almost every quarter  they've been buying back 15 to 25  billion worth of apple stock which just  reduces supply of apple stock  substantially within the last 10 years  the number of outstanding apple shares  has fallen from 26 billion shares to 16  billion shares this is one of the main  reasons that apple has been able to  continue growing their market cap  parabolically despite already being so  large aside from funding stock buybacks  another reason apple has so much debt is  to fund their dividends for the longest  time apple didn't actually pay any  dividends because they couldn't afford  to but starting in 2012 apple started  paying dividends and they've been  aggressively increasing their payouts  ever since their dividend payout has  literally increased 10x over the past 10  years now you might be wondering why  borrow money to pay out dividends well  the first thing to establish is that  apple does not need to borrow money to  pay dividends they just choose to and  the reason they choose to is to make  their stock more desirable here's the  thing even a company like apple can only  grow their revenues and profits so much  iphone sales peaked years ago and the  only reason apple has been able to  continue growing their numbers at an  insane rate is thanks to their high  margin services last quarter for example  service related profit accounted for  nearly half of their gross profit even  though services don't even account for a  quarter of their revenue so services  have really been their cash cow over the  past couple of years but sooner or later  that's gonna start leveling out as well  and at that point appeal of apple stock  would be lower than ever  now this is not a bad thing given that  all companies eventually get to decide  of the s-curve but in the meantime most  companies buy back stock and pump up  dividends to slot on this process as  much as possible  with the economic and technical  reasoning out of the way we have the  fundamental reasoning for taking out  debt honestly this isn't super  applicable to apple today given that  they're already past the growth phase i  mean it's not like apple needs to borrow  money to build a new factory or develop  the next big thing now technically apple  does spend a significant amount in both  of these areas but it's not like it  really grows the company all that much  for example they recently built a  billion-dollar campus in austin which is  great and all but it's not like it's  pivotal to their growth but just because  they can't use debt to fundamentally  grow themselves doesn't mean that they  can't use debt to fundamentally grow the  company as a whole the main way they  accomplish this is through a bunch of  acquisitions now this might be a bit  surprising because you almost never hear  about apple acquisitions on the news but  this isn't to say that they don't do  acquisitions they actually do a bunch of  acquisitions the reason you don't hear  about them is because apple stays away  from all the hot companies that you're  familiar with instead they buy several  background companies every year usually  for a few hundred million each in 2017  for example apple completed 12  acquisitions in 2018 they completed 10.  in 2019 they completed six and in 2020  they completed nine and that's just the  ones that are listed on wikipedia  according to apple insider apple usually  completes between 15 and 20 acquisitions  per year  usually these acquisitions help them  advance their current products or  develop new products for example in 2020  they acquired an ar company called  kamurai and a vr company called spaces  these acquisitions support the rumors  that apple will be coming out with their  own ar and vr products which will of  course help the company continue growing  fundamentally they're not limited to  just buying startups either they can go  out and buy stock in their partners or  even competitors if we take a look at  apple's balance sheet we'll see that  they have 131 billion dollars in  long-term marketable securities so it's  very possible that a portion of this has  gone towards buying the stocks and bonds  of other fortune 500 companies  technically apple does not need to  report a stake in a public company  unless it's larger than five percent so  apple could have a hundred billion  dollars taken microsoft and the 50  billion dollars taken tesla without  having to tell anyone knowing apple  though they probably avoid these popular  companies if they do have stakes in  public companies it's probably in a  background company like qualcomm while  we don't exactly know what these  investments are as these investments  grow so does apple's balance sheet and  at that point it's just a matter of time  until that growth is reflected in their  stock price so it's more than worth it  for apple to use debt to fund their  investments  in the end while apple is more leveraged  than ever before it's not exactly a bad  thing because it's extremely strategic  it's not like apple is taking on credit  card debt at 20 interest rates to buy a  new tv  rather apple is taking on debt at  historically low interest rates to  better navigate high inflation while  also pumping up their stock price both  technically and fundamentally and  honestly this is a brilliant way to  protect the long-term future of the  company and its shareholders with that  being said though apple does have to be  careful because there's a fine line  between smart debt and stupid debt and  it's not that hard to end up with too  much debt that ends up weighing down the  company i mean have you ever heard of  2008 so though apple has been playing  their cards well so far they'll have to  keep a close eye on the situation to  make sure it doesn't get out of hand but  that's just what i think would you be  willing to loan apple money comment that  down below  also drop a like if this video gave you  a different perspective on that and of  course consider checking out our discord  community suggests future video ideas  and consider subscribing and see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one  [Music]  you

